# 3. Material Foundations

## Income Is a Resource, Not a Developmental Ingredient

Poverty is consistently associated with developmental disadvantage, but poverty is not a single exposure.

It can mean food insecurity, overcrowding, unstable housing, unsafe neighborhoods, limited healthcare, fewer learning materials, unpredictable transportation, chronic stress, or reduced time. These factors cluster together, making it difficult to assign a single causal coefficient to "income."

Two broad mechanisms are especially useful.

The **Family Investment Model** emphasizes what additional resources make possible: better nutrition, safer housing, learning materials, childcare, healthcare, and potentially greater parental time.

The **Family Stress Model** emphasizes what economic insecurity does to caregivers: anxiety, depression, conflict, cognitive burden, reduced emotional bandwidth, and household instability.

The models are not mutually exclusive.

More money can buy resources and reduce stress at the same time.

But this does not mean that every increase in income produces a measurable improvement in every aspect of development.

### The Baby's First Years experiment

The Baby's First Years trial provides a crucial test because it moved beyond correlation.

Low-income mothers were randomly assigned shortly after childbirth to receive either approximately $333 per month or a nominal $20 monthly payment.

At age four, the researchers found no statistically significant treatment effects on preregistered measures of language, executive function, social-emotional behavior, brain activity, visual processing, pre-literacy, or developmental diagnoses. Earlier infancy assessments had found greater mid-to-high-frequency EEG power in the higher-cash group, but the developmental meaning and persistence of that neural difference remained uncertain.

This is not evidence that poverty is irrelevant.

It is evidence that **changing one part of a developmental ecology does not necessarily transform the whole system**.

The transfer increased resources and altered some spending and reading behavior, but it did not substantially change every other condition surrounding the family. The research dossier therefore recommends distinguishing poverty reduction from a particular cash-only treatment.

There is another important implication.

The effect of money may depend on where a family begins.

An additional amount of income may have a different meaning under severe deprivation than under moderate hardship or near-basic-security conditions. The relationship may include thresholds and substitution effects rather than a simple linear gradient.

Money matters.

But what matters developmentally is what money changes.

